@node CMacs Rig
@chapter CMacs Rig: A Private cmacs --gowl Session to Test Against

@cindex rig
@cindex end-to-end tests
@cindex headless compositor, testing against

The rig is a second cmacs, started as @samp{emacs --gowl -Q} on
wlroots' headless backend, with its own MCP server, its own D-Bus
session bus and real Wayland clients inside it.  Tests drive it from
the outside, over the real MCP socket, the way an agent or
@command{emacsctl} does.  Nothing is stubbed: a @code{tools/call} goes
through the socket, the publisher, the registry and the dispatch that a
user's call goes through; a text field is a real text-input-v3 client;
a window is a window.

It exists because features were landing with unit tests that called
the Lisp handler directly and stubbed the C primitives.  Two bugs were
found only when one test talked to a real socket: migrated Lisp tools
were not replacing their C twins, and a @code{tools/call} with no
@code{"arguments"} crashed the editor.  Neither is reachable from a
test that never leaves its own process.

Three files, all in @file{test/cmacs/}:

@table @file
@item cmacs-rig.el
The harness: start and stop the child, an MCP client,
@code{cmacs-rig-eval} and @code{cmacs-rig-do}, client spawning,
screenshots.
@item cmacs-rig-field.c
The text-field client: a Wayland window that is one text-input-v3
field, built by the test at run time.
@item cmacs-rig-tests.el
The ERT tests.
@end table

@menu
* Running the Rig::             The recipe, and why it is not in check-cmacs.
* Rig Isolation::               What keeps it off the live session.
* Rig Coverage::                What the tests assert.
* Writing a Rig Case::          The macro, the two callers, the rules.
