@node Running the Rig
@section Running It

@example
just test-rig                 # the whole file
just test-rig 'edit-field'    # tests whose name matches a regexp
@end example

@noindent
which is

@example
CMACS_RIG=1 src/emacs --batch -Q -L lisp/cmacs -L test/cmacs \
  --eval '(setq load-prefer-newer t)' -l ert \
  -l test/cmacs/cmacs-rig-tests.el -f ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit
@end example

A run takes about two minutes, most of it the sweep that calls every
migrated tool twice.

It is @emph{not} part of @samp{make -C test check-cmacs}.  That target
loads every @file{.el} in @file{test/cmacs/}, these two included, so
both must be harmless there: @file{cmacs-rig.el} defines no tests, and
every test in @file{cmacs-rig-tests.el} skips unless @env{CMACS_RIG} is
set in the environment.  It is separate because it is slower than the
unit suites, compiles a C program and launches processes, and needs
@command{gcc}, @command{pkg-config}, @command{wayland-scanner},
wayland-protocols, @command{dbus-daemon} and @command{timeout}.  A test
whose requirement is missing skips and says which.

@table @env
@item CMACS_RIG
Set to anything: run the tests.  Unset: every test skips.
@item CMACS_RIG_SHOTS
A directory.  Each scenario saves @file{@var{NN}-scenario-step.png}
there at its meaningful moments, and a test that does not pass saves
@file{@var{NN}-not-passed-@var{name}.png}.  Unset: no pictures.
@item CMACS_RIG_KEEP
Set: the rig's directory (log, client logs, dump files) is left behind
and its name printed.  A rig that fails to start is always kept.
@item CMACS_RIG_RENDERER
@env{WLR_RENDERER} of the child; default @samp{pixman}, which needs no
GPU.  @samp{gles2} exercises the effect modules where a render node
exists.
@item CMACS_RIG_DISABLE
Set: no rig starts (every test skips).
@end table

With @env{CMACS_RIG_SHOTS} set, @code{cmacs-rig-shot} saves a PNG of
the child's headless output.  The picture is taken @emph{inside} the
child, by its own @code{gowl-screenshot} of its own output, from its
main loop rather than over RPC, so it also shows the states a
model-facing screenshot is refused in.  The pixman renderer draws real
frames: the bar, the Emacs frame, the client windows and their text
are all there.  The field client draws its title and text when
pangocairo is installed and is a plain rectangle otherwise.

@node Rig Isolation
@section The Isolation Guarantees

The developer's desktop is itself a @samp{cmacs --gowl} session, so a
rig that reached it could kill it.  Each of these is enforced in
@file{cmacs-rig.el}, and @code{cmacs-rig-test-isolation} checks the
result from @file{/proc/@var{pid}/environ} of the child: what the
kernel says the child was started with, not what the harness believes
it passed.

@itemize
@item
@strong{The environment is built, not inherited.}  Five names are
copied (@env{PATH}, @env{TZ}, @env{USER}, @env{LOGNAME},
@env{LD_LIBRARY_PATH}); everything else is set by the harness.
@env{DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS} of the parent cannot leak: it is not on
the list.
@item
@strong{@env{WAYLAND_DISPLAY}, @env{WAYLAND_SOCKET} and @env{DISPLAY}
are explicitly unset.}  When @code{process-environment} has no entry
for @env{DISPLAY}, Emacs hands a subprocess the @env{DISPLAY} of
@code{initial-environment}, which is the live session's X server.  The
isolation test found exactly that in the first version of the harness.
@item
@strong{Private directories.}  @env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} (mode 0700),
@env{HOME} and the four XDG base directories are fresh directories
under one @code{make-temp-file} directory.  No user init, journal,
gabbrev or bar state is read or written.  The parent of that directory
is the user's runtime directory when there is one, because a socket
path has 108 bytes; the rig's runtime directory is a new directory
inside it, never it.
@item
@strong{Headless.}  @samp{WLR_BACKENDS=headless},
@samp{WLR_HEADLESS_OUTPUTS=1}, @samp{WLR_RENDERER=pixman},
@samp{GOWL_DISABLE_SYSTEMD=1}: the launch recipe of
@code{cmacs-gowl-tests--run-headless}.  No DRM, no parent compositor,
no input devices.
@item
@strong{A bus of its own.}  A @samp{dbus-daemon --session} listening
in the private runtime directory.  @env{NO_AT_BRIDGE},
@samp{GSETTINGS_BACKEND=memory}, @samp{GIO_USE_VFS=local} and
@env{GVFS_DISABLE_FUSE} keep the desktop's services from being
activated on it.
@item
@strong{It refuses before it starts.}  @code{cmacs-rig--assert-private}
signals @code{cmacs-rig-unsafe} if a computed directory is one of the
live session's, is outside the rig, is not 0700, already holds a
socket, is too long for a socket path, or if the environment names a
display or a bus that is not the rig's.  The tests treat that signal as
a failure, never as a skip.
@item
@strong{Clients see one display.}  A program started with
@code{cmacs-rig-spawn} gets the rig's environment plus the
@env{WAYLAND_DISPLAY} of the child compositor.
@item
@strong{Processes are known by PID and signalled by PID.}  Before a
PID that is not one of this Emacs's own process objects is signalled,
@file{/proc/@var{pid}/environ} must name the rig's runtime directory.
The last step of a stop sweeps every process of this user whose
environment does, which catches a program the child spawned itself.
Nothing is ever killed by name or pattern.
@item
@strong{Every wait has a deadline.}  Starting, each MCP request, each
poll.  The child ends itself when the Emacs that started it disappears,
and @command{timeout} ends it after @code{cmacs-rig-lifetime} seconds
whatever happens.
@item
@strong{On failure it says where the evidence is.}  The rig's
directory, the child's PID and the tail of its log are in every error
the harness signals.
@end itemize

Two things the rig does @emph{not} isolate: the network (a tool that
fetches from the internet will; the sweep leaves those out by name, see
@code{cmacs-rig-tests-sweep-excluded}), and @file{/tmp} (anything in
cmacs that writes to a fixed path there writes to the real one;
@code{cmacs-c-compile} no longer does, @code{cmacs-print}'s spool
directory still does).

@node Rig Coverage
@section What It Covers

@table @asis
@item The rig (@code{isolation}, @code{dbus-private-bus})
The child's environment and sockets are private; it serves
@samp{org.cmacs.Editor} on its own bus.
@item MCP wire (@code{wire-lists-migrated-tools}, @code{wire-no-argument-sweep}, @code{wire-tools-with-arguments})
Every migrated tool is listed once.  Every migrated tool with no
required parameter answers a real @code{tools/call}, with an empty
@code{"arguments"} and with none; the child survives.  A few tools
with arguments give the right answer.
@item Which side answers (@code{wire-registry-serves-migrated-tool}, @code{wire-eval-cannot-be-replaced})
A migrated tool is dispatched by the registry (the sweep checks this
for all of them); a registry tool called @code{eval} never replaces the
built-in.
@item Redaction (@code{redaction-*})
With a rule file in the child's own
@file{~/.config/cmacs/journal-filters.yaml}, an excluded window and
buffer appear in no model-facing answer over MCP or D-Bus, a screenshot
is refused while the window is visible, and a rule file that does not
load shows nothing at all.
@item Text fields (@code{text-field-*}, @code{edit-field-*})
@code{gowl-text-field-read} returns what the application holds and
@code{gowl-text-field-replace} changes it (non-ASCII, checked against
the application's own dump).  The edit-field command opens a floating
editor without moving any tiled window, and finishing writes back.
@item AI field (@code{ai-field-*})
Field text goes to the model and its answer comes back into the field;
the model is a function inside the child.  Focus moved meanwhile:
nothing is typed anywhere.
@item Journal (@code{journal-*})
Focus changes become spans in the child's own journal directory
(0700/0600); an agent sees none of it; a checkpoint lists the rig's
windows; a restore relaunches a closed window on its recorded tag.
@item LQL watch (@code{lql-watch-fires-once})
A watch on @code{buffers} fires once when a buffer appears and not on
idle ticks.
@item Deixis (@code{deixis-pointer-history})
The pointer ring answers ``where was it at time T'' with the right
window after injected motion.
@end table

@node Writing a Rig Case
@section Writing a Case

@lisp
(cmacs-rig-deftest my-feature ()
  "What must be true."
  (skip-unless (cmacs-rig-field-client))
  (let ((field (cmacs-rig-spawn-field rig :title "rig-mine"
                                      :text "hello")))
    (cmacs-rig-focus rig (plist-get field :id))
    (cmacs-rig-do rig '(my-command))
    (cmacs-rig-shot rig "my-feature-after")
    (cmacs-rig-wait-field-text rig field "HELLO")))
@end lisp

@code{rig} is bound in the body.  The macro skips without
@env{CMACS_RIG}, starts the shared rig if need be, resets it
afterwards, and asserts the child is still alive.

There are two callers, on purpose:

@table @code
@item cmacs-rig-call
@itemx cmacs-rig-tool-text
@itemx cmacs-rig-request
An MCP client: what an agent sees.
@item cmacs-rig-eval
The MCP @code{eval} tool, inside the RPC dispatch.  For reading state
and setting things up.  @code{inhibit-interaction} is bound and
redaction is in force.
@item cmacs-rig-do
A zero timer in the child's main loop: a command as the person at the
keyboard runs it.  No RPC, no redaction, not ``an agent''.
@item cmacs-rig-dbus
@command{gdbus} on the rig's bus: the D-Bus surface.
@end table

The difference is the point of several tests.
@code{cmacs-journal-events} answers @code{cmacs-rig-do} and returns
nothing to @code{cmacs-rig-eval}; the redaction functions are the
identity for one and active for the other.  A command tested only
through @code{cmacs-rig-eval} is tested as an agent would run it, which
is not how it is reached.

The MCP @code{eval} tool evaluates with dynamic binding: a
@code{lambda} in a form sent through @code{cmacs-rig-eval} closes over
nothing.  @code{cmacs-rig-do} evaluates lexically.

The rules:

@itemize
@item
The child never waits.  Poll from the parent with
@code{cmacs-rig-wait-for}; never send a form that sleeps.
@item
Name everything a test creates @samp{rig-...}: the reset kills buffers
by that prefix and closes every window that is not the child's own
frame.
@item
A test for a bug that is not fixed yet is marked
@code{:expected-result :failed} with a comment naming the bug.  The
file stays green, and turns red (``unexpectedly passed'') the day the
bug is fixed and the marker should go.
@item
A value returned through @code{cmacs-rig-eval} must print readably:
select fields rather than returning a client object.
@end itemize

@node Rig Field Client
@section The Text-Field Client

@file{cmacs-rig-field.c} speaks text-input-v3 itself and uses no
toolkit.  A GTK entry was the first attempt and cannot work in the rig:
GTK enables its text input only while its window has keyboard focus,
which it learns from @code{wl_keyboard.enter}, and a headless gowl has
no keyboard device, so its seat advertises no @code{wl_keyboard} at
all.  A virtual keyboard did not change that either.  Both are fixed
in gowl (a virtual keyboard counts, and @env{GOWL_SEAT_KEYBOARD=always}
forces the capability; @file{deps/gowl/docs/configuration.org},
@emph{Machines without a keyboard}), so a toolkit client can now be
hosted once the rig sets that variable; the field client stays
toolkit-free because it needs no toolkit.

What it does is what a toolkit does: on @code{enter} it enables the
text input and reports its surrounding text; on
@code{delete_surrounding_text}, @code{commit_string} and @code{done} it
applies one edit; on @code{leave} it disables.  It rewrites a dump file
whenever the text changes, which is what the @emph{application} holds,
as opposed to what the compositor believes.

By default it sends the compositor a @code{wl_display.sync} every 20
ms, as a desktop with anything alive on it does.  @code{:idle t} turns
that off; the difference is a bug (@pxref{Rig Findings}).

@node Rig Findings
@section What It Found

The first runs found these.  Each has a test marked
@code{:expected-result :failed}, or is noted where no test could be
written.

@itemize
@item
@code{gowl-text-field-read} and @code{gowl-text-field-replace} do not
flush the client after entering the borrowed text input.  On an idle
desktop the @code{enter} is written only when the operation's own 250
ms timeout turns the event loop, which then gives up: about half the
calls report @code{no-field}.  Test:
@code{text-field-read-on-an-idle-desktop}.
@item
@code{cmacs-gowl-ai-field--withheld-p} asks @code{cmacs-redact-window}
without @code{cmacs-redact-as-model}.  From a key binding the redaction
functions are the identity, so an excluded window's field is sent to
the model and rewritten.  Test:
@code{ai-field-excluded-window-is-withheld}.
@item
D-Bus @code{Compositor.ListClients} and @code{Compositor.FocusedClient}
are composed in C and never redacted.  Test:
@code{redaction-dbus-compositor-clients}.
@item
D-Bus @code{BufferManager.List} names excluded buffers.  Test:
@code{redaction-dbus-buffer-list}.
@item
No migrated tool sets @code{destructiveHint}, @code{delete_window}
included; the sweep's exclusion list exists because of it.
@item
@code{gnuseye_list_layers} and its siblings fetch textures from the
network when first called; they are excluded from the sweep by name.
@emph{Fixed:} nothing is fetched until a globe is open
(@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-load-quiet-tests.el}); the exclusion can go.
@item
A headless or keyboard-less seat advertises no @code{wl_keyboard}, even
with a virtual keyboard: injected keys reach no client.  This is why
the field client uses no toolkit.  @emph{Fixed in gowl:} a virtual
keyboard counts, and @env{GOWL_SEAT_KEYBOARD=always} forces the
capability (@file{deps/gowl/tests/test-seat-keyboard.c},
@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-gowl-seat-keyboard-tests.el}).
@end itemize
